[Music]  in your cover story and in your book you  talk about a war on the west what kind  of shape is that war taking  in my view what's happening is a  vicious unrelenting remorseless attack  on everything that constitutes the west  as we know it  the west's history the peoples of the  west the culture of the west all the  institutions of the west the traditions  of the west  and this is this has been growing for  quite a long time now you could argue  it's been growing for the best part of  at least half a century and then it has  sped up massively in the last few years  and as i say in my cover story in the  spectator this week  one of the most visible things is this  war on the history of the west the war  on the history of a country like britain  so that you know every single one of our  institutions from  kew gardens to  um  the national trust  to the tate to the british library to um  the globe theater all seem to be all the  things that are meant to be effectively  custodians of our history have actually  started to wage a war on that history so  that they present our history as if  there's just nothing good about it and  as i say in my cover piece one of the  many devastating things about this is  that a country and a culture and a  society can only be held together if  first of all it has some agreement on  its past and secondly  it thinks that past is broadly good  because if you don't think you were good  in the past why would you do anything in  the present right but is this so much  different to what we had during the cold  war for example i mean and america was  hugely divided to come i remember seeing  some figures recently about how many  domestic bombings there were in america  in a typical year in the 1960s in  britain you had full-on communist  parties in america youth communist  parties in britain who were making um  absolutely devastating critiques uh well  not very effective but they were you  know very much at war with capitalism  with the basics of today  is it really  but now we've just got a few students  pulling down statues i mean is it really  worse  well it started with students and as  andrew sullivan famously said it's built  out and now we all live on campus  it's now gone through every corporation  every government department it isn't  just a few students anymore there was a  time when you could argue that it was  but the students left university and  they and they went right out into the  marketplace and they had jobs to go to  you know one of the jokes on  conservatives in recent years was that  conservatives often joked in the last  generation these people doing these  ridiculous degrees they're gonna go out  into the real world they'll discover  there aren't any jobs for them the  market will assert itself flat out wrong  the joke was on us there were plenty of  jobs for these people to go on into they  went into hr departments they they went  into the civil service they were the  ones who passed the civil services  demand that for any public appointment  any civil service appointment you have  to demonstrate a belief in and and  devotion to diversity inclusion equity  all things which are in their  fundamentals as i say in the piece  western you cannot dedicate yourself to  this new pseudo-ideological pseudo  religion because in part everything  about it is against the institutions and  the history of what the country used to  believe in until yesterday so you know  the big difference between now and the  cold war was first of all in the cold  war there was some unity because there  was some recognition of a shared foe but  secondly there were still adults there  were still adults in the room willing to  stand up for traditions willing to stand  up for history willing to say that  actually we have virtues as a country  and not just vices virtues as a people  not just vices things that we did well  in our history not just things we did  ill and today look around the public  square  all of the adults fled the scene  every corporation in britain every  government department every publishing  house everybody sees the same thing the  people you describe as the sort of  highly ideological students make all of  the running the shouting the desecration  of the past and the adults give in  effectively what we are living through  now in britain and then the wider west  is a cultural revolution and it's a  cultural revolution where the adults  have been intimidated into either  silence or going along with it  but douglas you say there was a common  enemy in the cold war but we're getting  one now aren't we since putin's invasion  of ukraine there has been a strikingly  coherent and as far as i can work out  spontaneous response from europe and  america and beyond into presenting a  united front against him  well  it's a very important point you just  made  the russian invasion of ukraine seems to  some extent to have have fortified  certain people it seems to have  clarified the minds of some people it's  answered the 30 year old question that  we all asked of wither nato what's the  point of nato that question isn't being  asked anymore when sweden and other  countries join nato later this year that  will be a very interesting answer in  itself but  here is the problem it's not just  putin's russia in the 21st century that  poses a threat a far bigger threat a far  bigger challenge in the 21st century is  china the communist party of china which  as i mentioned in the book is the only  country in the world putin's russia is a  basket case it was even before this war  especially is after it the only country  in the 21st century likely to catch up  with and then supersede america and the  western nations as the global power the  most important economy in the world is  china led by the chinese communist party  and that pumps out anti-western  propaganda all the time just this past  week the china daily news one of the  organs of the chinese communist party um  pumped out a vision of uncle sam behind  the oval office desk and a pile of  corpses and said that america is all  about racism and and so on there's two  points to make here the first is of  course this is totally cynical i mean  ask any of the muslims in xinjiang  whether the chinese communist party  actually minds about family separations  or racism and you'd get an answer but  secondly the problem that exists now is  not chinese anti-westernism or russian  anti-westernism or or middle eastern  anti-westernism it is western  anti-westernism it is white people doing  this against white people it is white  people  deciding that our history is all a  history of racism and that we're racist  now and institutionally racist now and  somehow derek chovin who was convicted  of the killing of george floyd is the  emblematic figure of the west and this  isn't a fringe thing you know no less a  figure than the u.s  ambassador to the un went to the u.n  last year and condemned her own country  for institutional racism something which  by the way the chinese communist parties  envoy to the un found incredibly useful  when he stood up to make his remarks and  said this is the first time that the  country has come to the united nations  and confessed its guilt  so the problem is not the external  challenge the problem is the internal  one the problem is people in the west  have been taught to think horribly about  the west so that our history is only a  history of repression and slavery and  colonialism our history is uniquely bad  not uniquely good here's here's the most  important point in a way and it's  something i bring across not just in the  book but in in my peaceful spectator  this week we have evidence for the fact  that this is flat out wrong how do we  know because the football proves it the  un published another study just in the  last year asking people around the world  fleeing not just conflict but economic  deprivation where do you want to go what  are your destinations people do not flee  to putin's russia they do not flee to  venezuela they do not flee to communist  china the number one destination that  people around the world want to go to is  the united states of america and britain  and canada and others follow shortly  afterwards now that says something it  says that whilst our society is being  told we have nothing to be proud of the  world wants to come here that whilst we  are being told we live in a racist  society and a bewildering array of  people want to come here and here's the  important thing about that it's not just  in the present  our present is not as it is by accident  it's come about because of our past if  there's something good in our present  it's because we must have done something  good in the past and that's what i want  people to realize and to be able to  focus on if we had to unite as a country  as a civilization as a culture we need  to recognize not just that we've done  some things well but we've be done more  things well than anybody else and it's  proved in the footfall of the people who  want to come here and one final thing  douglas isn't another thing we do well  arguing with each other i mean you talk  about these divisions and this huge  cantankerousness but isn't that the sign  of a west succeeding i mean there isn't  much war institutions in north korea  right now there's not much in the way of  public debate wouldn't you expect a  successful thriving west to be  absolutely at loggerheads over culture  music history the whole gamut  absolutely and as i say in the book i  absolutely encourage that i want to shut  down no debate but currently we have had  an entirely one-sided debate in recent  years there was a time when the debate  was one-sided in the opposite direction  few generations ago if you were taught  about colonialism in british schools you  were taught only the good things um a  few generations ago if you studied  american history you might not have  known that thomas jefferson owned slaves  today it's the only thing people know  about the founding fathers in america so  here's the thing i want there to be a  rigorous debate i want there to be a  thriving debate but i do not want it to  be one-sided and the people who have  been attacking white people in recent  years and being saying racist things  about white people have been a one-sided  monotone discussion and they've got away  with it for too long the people who've  attacked our culture and all of our  heroes from winston churchill to lord  nelson these people have been doing a  one-sided attack on our history i  believe that the british public and the  wider western public have the right to  have a more honest appraisal of  ourselves and our past and that includes  the right  to have pride in our past because as it  happens it's not just vital for a  society to hold together but the facts  just happen to be on our side  douglas thank you for your appraisal and  for your cover story in this week's  magazine  [Music]  you

